{"title":"1994 USAMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/7/f47b7cc4445cb760dcbe4147a01f7fc39d0b6c12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, k_1 &lt; k_2 &lt; k_3 &lt;\\cdots\\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"146\" height=\"15\" />, be positive integers, no two consecutive, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f13573df6bdd68cbed903730a89171e388194ed4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, s_m = k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_m\\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"191\" height=\"15\" />, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/1/1413e411ce31dcbfee702305ddb9fcb6a6f99e81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, m = 1,2,3,\\ldots\\;\\;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"114\" height=\"16\" />. Prove that, for each positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/d/23d82b93e5d3ccfe5935e75084b94c5d88cb38b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, [s_n, s_{n+1})\\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" />, contains at least one perfect square.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We want to show that the distance between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/3/2a321ec9a222994495cc22462296a6d0f0a071a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> is greater than the distance between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and the next perfect square following <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/8/41842bebae45d8ba3ef7bb287d8e15a1663be577.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"84\" height=\"48\" />, where no <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/0/9a0ac59ec3562b84367674cfe6245f223b892913.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" /> are consecutive, we can put a lower bound on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/2/fd26dda3f4f507f3fdfaff4fe0b468bee54e7c09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" />. This occurs when all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/685ae23481e42d785f8d9225ef0f5b0789dbbd26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_{i+1}=k_i+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"102\" height=\"16\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/0/b30a9b949c01103335adbed0d99534d070114415.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} s_n&amp;=(k_{n,min})+(k_{n,min}-2)+(k_{n,min}-4)+\\dots+(k_{n,min}-2n+2)\\\\ &amp;=nk_{n,min}-\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}2i\\\\ &amp;=nk_{n,min}-2\\sum_{i=1}^{n}i+2n\\\\ &amp;=nk_{n,min}-n(n+1)+2n\\\\ &amp;=nk_{n,min}-n^2+n\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"531\" height=\"190\" />\n</p><p>Rearranging, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/6/3d6e5fbe752fc8b41f47beb18d942f662062c36a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_{n,min}=\\frac{s_n}{n}+n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"156\" height=\"33\" />. So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/f/6bfc52c0215b1aba1789c38e009fdef68f64bfe1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_n\\geq\\frac{s_n}{n}+n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"127\" height=\"33\" />, and the distance between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/3/2a321ec9a222994495cc22462296a6d0f0a071a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/6/91669506a1c44de4675ac05b2bbde1d81e6da100.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_{n+1}\\geq k_n+2\\geq\\frac{s_n}{n}+n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"218\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p><p>Also, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/6/ce6b5a121b68e68db267d3765f74138c354e1f6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(s_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> be the distance between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and the next perfect square following <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" />. Let's look at the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/0/940d22a199a4ed236a98b82ab364343eada11f47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> for all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a perfect square, it is easy to see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/8/cb8bfbac651d59052268413bb2953ec6d6f9f415.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(x)=2\\sqrt{x}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"19\" />.\nProof: Choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f8c182a4e5355ed871855bfb67048b1160954f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=m^2$\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/3/8337234da19ae03b1704a3719c254f14008e9051.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(m^2)=(m+1)^2-m^2=2m+1=2\\sqrt{m^2}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"368\" height=\"22\" />.\n</p><p>When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is not a perfect square, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/2/082540db09ca9c7714c2c3e7106c216c51d5a67c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(x)&lt;2\\sqrt{x}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"19\" />.\nProof: Choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/8/5c803e16479a9ba61908531b60a223e88ba1524b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=m^2+p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/0/d5079204ade0fe2c5668fbd771c12e8e6e33967a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;p&lt;2m+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"122\" height=\"16\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/b/17b631ea425b05fac38429565c6c1dcde800d3a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(m^2+p)=(m+1)^2-m^2-p=2m+1-p&lt;2m+1=2\\sqrt{m^2}+1&lt;2\\sqrt{m^2+p}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"680\" height=\"22\" />.\n</p><p>So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/8/4b87a37f7597337445dcd7f2bbcc6e095b028b99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(x)\\leq 2\\sqrt{x}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"123\" height=\"19\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/c/7ac76e47d219fe82d26870ac626cfb2af3af362b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(s_n)\\leq 2\\sqrt{s_n}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"137\" height=\"18\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Now, it suffices to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/e/03ef043493971b025e99e989daef81583193d0ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_{n+1}\\geq d(s_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/9/729f5c7cebb201fc728b5d52f3235c8769a3887b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} k_{n+1}-d(s_n)&amp;\\geq \\frac{s_n}{n}+n+1-2\\sqrt{s_n}-1\\\\ &amp;=\\frac{1}{n}(s_n+n^2-2n\\sqrt{s_n})\\\\ &amp;=\\frac{s_n^2+n^4+2n^2s_n-4n^2s_n}{n(s_n+n^2+2n\\sqrt{s_n})}\\\\ &amp;=\\frac{(s_n-n^2)^2}{n(s_n+n^2+2n\\sqrt{s_n})}\\\\ &amp;\\geq 0 \\end{align*}\" width=\"317\" height=\"201\" />\n</p><p>So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/e/03ef043493971b025e99e989daef81583193d0ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_{n+1}\\geq d(s_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" /> and all intervals between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/3/2a321ec9a222994495cc22462296a6d0f0a071a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> will contain at least one perfect square.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We see that by increasing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> by some amount, we simply shift our interval by a finite amount. It suffices to consider the case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> (since this can be inducted across all positive integers). Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/e/58e45cec9c956d417f05b1161d9cfd6dbef19c6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_1=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" />. We want the smallest interval, so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/4/f447779a86e6d4e19ff07dc7eba7d200a79abed6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[x, 2x+2]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />. Simple induction reveals that the ration of consecutive squares grows slower than our linear bound. We now consider sufficiently small <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/b/f7b95c786816678f81a106ab616de604a8af40bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(n+1)^2}{n^2}&lt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"100\" height=\"39\" />). This first happens at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />. By simple casework, our answer is as desired <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We will first prove by Induction on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a64508262bb2272e129bd59c31142b17e78b6ad3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/6/e164ad89533becc0f076b11bd29cee62bbc44c39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(k_{n}+1)^2\\geq4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots +k_{n}).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"262\" height=\"19\" /> Denote this statement by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c326ff72243d233ee4b0130c17f7343b98b48a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(n).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>For the Base Case let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/3/5e351ab9d918e64c81a4620b6c9054301762a7b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> we know that;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/4/ac4733fa6655c2d916acee04f546adef37cf8b71.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(k_1-1)^2\\geq 0\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/7/3779d40fcf679bea9bcd9bfb86ea3d3e6633eaf6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_1^2-2k_1+1\\geq 0\\]\" width=\"152\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/f/72f2c2a50bc369c7d427d2b99aeadb35a2f34dec.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_1^2+2k_1+1\\geq 4k_1\\]\" width=\"167\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/8/ae88edac1941da6db2f95101da07cc56959c195f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow (k_1+1)^2\\geq 4k_1.\\]\" width=\"146\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Hence, the Base Case holds.\n</p><p>For the Inductive Step, suppose that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/f/aff8348b511005a0c641105c61b322a05d80704b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(m)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"46\" height=\"20\" /> holds for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/9/3091b02f2cb69a08c1baa8eeb4fa3cbbccc351cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\in\\mathbb{N},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"49\" height=\"16\" /> we will prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/d/bcdbd7985705e241c8721a16d323a2243c1b4a06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(m+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> holds as well.\n</p><p>Assume for contradiction that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/d/bcdbd7985705e241c8721a16d323a2243c1b4a06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(m+1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"18\" /> doesn't hold, then we know that;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/b/11b192686e947787b0e28b9ce4112c6e4244869f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(k_m+1)^2&lt;4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots +k_m)\\]\" width=\"263\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/f/06f0464e5c1ffdc2c0e263a131694f6b189b8fb4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_m^2+2k_m+1&lt;4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots +k_{m-1})+4k_m\\]\" width=\"389\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/a/19a2e591e97cdb7ae5534d4b44fbf3724c0ab49d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_m^2-2k_m+1&lt;4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots +k_{m-1})\\]\" width=\"335\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/8/7386a3e0d2d7f51071c7b8e0eccae5eceba9c1f8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow (k_m-1)^2&lt;4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots +k_{m-1}).\\]\" width=\"309\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>We know that since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/1/a91b89ebe13c1b72626dbf60958e6a5179e20711.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/d/fddef2919697ac936aff68edd36a2d8df92ac5d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_{m-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" /> are not consecutive, we have;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/4/1544a957715970b0698eececa20512732d2b0762.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_{m-1}\\geq k_m-2\\]\" width=\"115\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/9/f39b4449009209fbd8a82b719e406c95a7695b74.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_{m-1}+1\\leq k_m-1\\]\" width=\"169\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/9/c699b6bcec2e226a06b774818343d2a38e1880ba.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow (k_{m-1}+1)^2\\leq (k_m-1)^2\\]\" width=\"212\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/9/769353f11a8d7bac182ed36820c1fe1f9b06ce1c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow (k_{m-1}+1)^2\\leq (k_m-1)^2&lt;4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots +k_{m-1}).\\]\" width=\"425\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>But this contradicts the Inductive Hypothesis that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/3/f138a0e005994e2d3162411627b71bae2b3bebe0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow (k_{m-1}+1)^2\\geq 4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots +k_{m-1}),\\]\" width=\"326\" height=\"19\" /> thus our assumption was false and the Inductive Step is complete.\n</p><p>Hence, we have proved that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7dfd17dc4d3100462df726e8eb7f9262783429eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> holds, and we will use <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7dfd17dc4d3100462df726e8eb7f9262783429eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> to solve the original problem.\n</p><p><br />\nNow suppose that for some positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/d/23d82b93e5d3ccfe5935e75084b94c5d88cb38b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, [s_n, s_{n+1})\\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" /> does not contain any perfect square, then we know that there must exist two perfect squares of consecutive integers, such that the smaller one is lesser than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24f09efdef5385985d3f51f9163958f3aaa64a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> and the larger one greater than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/b/e1bccec49cd23896b1ffca436c0c9b86daca2b26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_{n+1}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>We thus know that there exists some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/a/faa9556fe5e881a4a7fda5393b04f61f8bdd13a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> such that;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/5/1f5e816610cd92dd8b308ebd93afc49d97a44b44.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{eqnarray} x^2&lt;s_n \\\\ \\text{and }(x+1)^2\\geq s_{n+1} \\end{eqnarray}\" width=\"332\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p>By Inequality 1;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/b/2bbc09abaccedf351b7fd9dbd8c711b507fe2a11.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} x &amp;&lt;\\sqrt{s_n}\\\\ &amp;=\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}.\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"195\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p><br />\nHence, we know that;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/1/861b9f7e48e2925e534b947d12f094a97b3af8de.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} (x+1)^2 &amp;&lt;(\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}+1)^2\\\\ &amp;=k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n+2\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}+1. \\end{align*}\" width=\"450\" height=\"52\" />\n</p><p>Combining this with Inequality 2 gives;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/a/c7a93d5bfc9db2c7417c8baeeed0ec6d87240a2f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[s_{n+1}\\leq (x+1)^2&lt;k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n+2\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}+1\\]\" width=\"478\" height=\"22\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/4/d64f44afc6600ba54a645e2a852e496b9fd9994e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n+k_{n+1}&lt;k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n+2\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}+1\\]\" width=\"531\" height=\"22\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/7/7e7e666c234b008afe76678734ad35a1678ae06a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_{n+1}&lt;2\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}+1\\]\" width=\"279\" height=\"22\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/a/46afe1b1fee727b42ff413d5cc5f1d6e5c2a346c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow k_{n+1}-1&lt;2\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}.\\]\" width=\"283\" height=\"22\" />\n</p><p><br />\nWe know that since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/c/f3c2d031a0f3a29766b3caacec51addb76897a62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" /> are not consecutive, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/7/ed79ae7f77c84f22b2eeb7ec26cc5997d986e809.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_n+1\\leq k_{n+1}-1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"144\" height=\"16\" /> hence, we have;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/d/a3d45b1b5490d5cc8284c8624e5fd4e7db233468.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_{n+1}\\leq k_{n+1}-1&lt;2\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}\\]\" width=\"317\" height=\"21\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/2/7e275210f9bda21e7f054befca90d615d380b8d6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_{n+1}&lt;2\\sqrt{k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n}\\]\" width=\"226\" height=\"22\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/9/4f9d8f1d4d26925caa8421c358a2c8d7ebaa91ea.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(k_{n+1}^2&lt;4(k_1+k_2+\\cdots+k_n).\\]\" width=\"232\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><br />\nBut this contradicts the statement <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/f/7dfd17dc4d3100462df726e8eb7f9262783429eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> which was proved earlier.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO\" title=\"1994 USAMO\">1994 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1994 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1994\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First Problem</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1994 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1994 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1994 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1994 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1994_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1994 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017184733\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.052 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.076 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 520/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1237/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 51/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 18115/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,